  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">A.D. Carson\u2019s <em>Owning My Masters (Mastered)</em> is already showing early scholarly and public reach. The book has 6 OpenAlex citations, 35 recorded downloads, and one documented teaching-use signal in a New Books Network video, suggesting the work is beginning to circulate across research, access, and instructional contexts. It is also published as gold open access, which likely supports that early visibility.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published by the University of Michigan Press in 2024 as part of the Tracking Pop series, <em>Owning My Masters (Mastered)</em> is an open-access scholarly book by A.D. Carson. The record places the book in a history-related conceptual area, and its citation count indicates an emerging scholarly footprint rather than a mature one. With 6 citations so far, the book is beginning to be taken up in academic work, but the evidence remains early and concentrated.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation landscape is modest but suggestive. Most citing works are associated with education and the United States, and the visible citations cluster in music-related and cultural fields, including music history and musicology. The one clearly identified citing article appears in <em>Traditions of Music and Dance</em>, pointing to disciplinary uptake in adjacent humanities and music studies rather than broad diffusion across many fields.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Usage data also show some reader engagement: 35 downloads have been recorded through OAPEN, with activity spread across several months. Because downloads capture access rather than citation or endorsement, they should be read as a sign of readership and discoverability, not as a direct measure of scholarly influence. The book\u2019s open-access availability through OAPEN and the University of Michigan\u2019s Fulcrum platform likely supports that access.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">There is also a small but meaningful teaching signal: the book was discussed in a New Books Network video, which suggests attention in a format often used by instructors, students, and general academic audiences. Beyond that, the available record is relatively quiet. That silence should be read cautiously: it does not imply lack of impact, only that the currently indexed evidence is still limited. Overall, the book shows an early pattern of scholarly uptake, some instructional visibility, and access-enabled readership, with room for wider influence to accumulate over time.</p>",
  "narrative_source": "template"
